# Palisade — Build Memory

## Visual Flair — Atmosphere + Props Layer (2026-06-16)

Optional dungeon polish lives entirely in `scripts/VisualFlair.cs`, gated behind a
**control panel** of static fields (master `VisualFlair.Enabled` + per-feature
toggles/values read at build time). Wired in via two guarded one-liners only:
- `DungeonBuilder.Build()` → `VisualFlair.Decorate(this, data.Pieces, TorchByCell)`
- `OnlineDungeonArena.BuildEnvironment()` → `VisualFlair.ApplyEnvironment(env)`

To revert: set `Enabled = false` (runtime), or delete the file + those two calls.

What it does: exponential depth fog + glow/bloom + exposure grade; relocates each
corridor omni torch onto a real wall with an iron bracket + emissive flame cone;
1-in-`ShadowEvery` torches *can* cast shadows (**now OFF by default** — see perf note);
layered-sine flicker via the self-contained `TorchFlicker` node; tames the raw
3.5-energy Start(green)/Exit(red) landmark glows to ~2.0/1.6; fakes god-ray beams
at Start/Exit with an additive cone + spotlight (**true volumetric fog/SSAO are
NOT available in GL Compatibility** — must fake); plus hanging banners + floor rubble.

**Perf (measured 2026-06-18, dense 51-torch maze):** flair ON ~5× the GPU render
time of flair OFF, and **~80% of that increase is the omni shadows alone** (the rest
— fog/glow/props/flicker — is cheap). GL-compat point-light shadows are the costly
part and the online arena builds TWO mazes, doubling the caster count. Pure local
render cost — does NOT affect netcode/latency/bandwidth, only framerate/feel.
So `TorchShadows = false` by default; raise `ShadowEvery` or flip it on for richer
depth on strong GPUs. Harness supports `-- big shadowsoff` args for A/B perf runs.

Preview harness: `test/TestVisualFlair.cs` (+ `.tscn`) builds a sample corridor and
flies an eye-level camera through it. Pass `-- flairoff` to capture a before/after
from one build. Banners hang high on the 14m walls and need a brightened albedo +
faint emissive to read in the dim torchlight.

